---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Container from '../components/layout/Container.astro';
import PitfallRow from '../components/marketing/PitfallRow.astro';
import WaitlistForm from '../components/forms/WaitlistForm.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="The five pitfalls of operational readiness"
  description="Operational readiness fails the same five ways: siloed trackers, missed dependencies, setup paralysis, stranded handover docs, and notification fatigue. Here's how Runway eases each lift."
>
  <Section tone="cream" pad="default">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <header class="head stack-l">
        <p class="eyebrow">The breakdown</p>
        <h1 class="display step-5">Operational readiness fails the same five ways. Runway is built around fixing each.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          We didn't invent these pitfalls — we lived them on terminal handovers, resort openings, and
          critical-systems cutovers. Every feature in Runway maps back to one of these failure modes.
        </p>
      </header>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="paper" pad="default">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <PitfallRow num="01" pitfall="Siloed trackers across consultants.">
        <div slot="problem">
          <p>
            The risk register lives in the construction manager's Excel. Training requirements live
            in the operator's MS Project. Spec deviations live in a third-party PDF that's three
            revisions behind. Every status meeting is a reconciliation exercise — and the
            reconciliation drifts the moment the meeting ends.
          </p>
          <p>
            Worse, the same risk shows up in two trackers with different IDs, different owners, and
            different mitigations. The team can't tell which version is current.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div slot="solution">
          <p>
            One workspace. Every risk, issue, task, training event, procedure, and trial lives in a
            single tenant with typed relationships between them. Consultants get scoped access to
            their construction phase. Operators see the rollup. No one is reconciling.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <strong>My Work</strong> feed shows everyone their assigned items across modules in one
            list — color-coded by type, filtered by overdue, ranked by due date.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p slot="evidence">Evidence in Runway: unified EntityLink graph · scoped construction-phase membership · My Work cross-module feed</p>
      </PitfallRow>

      <PitfallRow num="02" pitfall="Missed dependencies between trades.">
        <div slot="problem">
          <p>
            &ldquo;We didn't know BHS commissioning needed the IT cutover to finish first.&rdquo; That sentence,
            spoken three weeks before opening day, is what kills ORAT programs. Every trade has a
            schedule. None of them talk to each other. When a date slips, no one knows what else
            just slipped with it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div slot="solution">
          <p>
            Runway links activities with typed dependencies (<em>Finish Before</em>, <em>Related To</em>,
            <em>Spawned From</em>) and offsets (14 days, 30 days, working-day-aware). When a predecessor
            moves, the offset engine recalculates every downstream date — and the timeline shows a
            chevron pointing at the driving predecessor, so the team can see <em>why</em> a date moved.
          </p>
          <p>
            The dependency graph on every milestone blade makes "what blocks this?" answerable
            without leaving the screen.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p slot="evidence">Evidence in Runway: ReadinessOffsetEngine · driving-predecessor chevron · EntityLink with offsets · dependency graph panel</p>
      </PitfallRow>

      <PitfallRow num="03" pitfall="Six weeks of project setup paralysis.">
        <div slot="problem">
          <p>
            Before anyone does real ORAT work, someone has to populate functional areas, space types,
            facility types, stakeholder roles, work item categories, training requirements, and
            specification rows. Each pulled from a different document. Each typed by hand. Each
            second-guessed in setup meetings.
          </p>
          <p>By the time setup is done, the project has lost six weeks.</p>
        </div>
        <div slot="solution">
          <p>
            Upload your ConOps. Upload your spec PDF. Upload your master program. Runway's
            Claude-powered Project Assistant reads them and proposes functional areas, stakeholders,
            training items, and spec rows as one-click suggestions. You accept what fits and skip
            what doesn't.
          </p>
          <p>
            For airports, resorts, healthcare, and data centers, project-type templates pre-seed
            sensible libraries so you start with a working set, not an empty database.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p slot="evidence">Evidence in Runway: ProjectAssistant · six AI suggestion modules · project-type library templates · spec PDF + Excel ingestion (JCB-378)</p>
      </PitfallRow>

      <PitfallRow num="04" pitfall="Stranded handover documents.">
        <div slot="problem">
          <p>
            Procedures live in the commissioning agent's OneDrive. Training records live in the
            operator's HR system. As-built drawings live in a SharePoint nobody can find. When
            handover lands, half the documents are stranded — and the operator's first week is
            spent hunting them down.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div slot="solution">
          <p>
            Runway doesn't store documents in its own database. Every attachment lives in <em>your</em>
            SharePoint via Microsoft Graph. When the construction team rolls off, the documents
            don't roll off with them — they were always in the operator's tenant.
          </p>
          <p>
            Create Word, Excel, and PowerPoint files directly from a procedure or training record
            without leaving Runway. The file is born in the right SharePoint library.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p slot="evidence">Evidence in Runway: SharePointDocumentService · Microsoft Graph attachment flow · "Create new SharePoint document" action (JCB-357)</p>
      </PitfallRow>

      <PitfallRow num="05" pitfall="Notification fatigue burying critical signals.">
        <div slot="problem">
          <p>
            One project-wide email distribution list. Every risk, every issue, every status change
            blasts to everyone. Within a week, the team filters Runway emails to "later." Critical
            risk escalations are read three days late, if at all.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div slot="solution">
          <p>
            Per-functional-area Teams webhook routing. Aquatics-area risks post to the Aquatics
            Teams channel. BHS issues post to the BHS channel. Cross-cutting events fall back to a
            project-wide channel. The signal-to-noise ratio collapses from "all" to "yours."
          </p>
          <p>
            For people who live in calendars instead of Teams, each user gets a signed iCal feed
            with their tasks, training, and owned milestones — works for external stakeholders who
            can't even reach Graph.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p slot="evidence">Evidence in Runway: per-FA Teams webhook routing (JCB-372) · per-user iCal feeds (JCB-365) · adaptive-card event allowlist</p>
      </PitfallRow>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="ink">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <div class="cta-block stack-l">
        <p class="eyebrow">If any of these sound familiar</p>
        <h2 class="display step-3">We'd like to talk to you.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Runway is in active development with a small first cohort. Add your email — we'll show you the parts that apply to your project.</p>
        <WaitlistForm source="pitfalls" showMessage cta="Get in touch" />
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .step-5 { font-size: var(--step-5); }
  .step-3 { font-size: var(--step-3); }
  .head { max-width: 50rem; }
  .cta-block { text-align: center; align-items: center; }
  .cta-block .lede { text-align: center; margin-inline: auto; }
  .cta-block .eyebrow { color: var(--accent-soft); }
  .cta-block :global(.waitlist) { max-width: 28rem; margin-inline: auto; width: 100%; }
</style>
